## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives, "change" means a different value. For objects, arrays, and functions, it means a different **reference** — even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a NEW object reference → child always re-renders
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />;  // {} !== {} each render
}

// Same problem with inline functions
function Parent() {
  return <Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />;  // new fn reference each render
}
```

This is because `{} === {}` is `false` in JavaScript. React's default shallow comparison (`Object.is`) sees a new reference and triggers a re-render.

---

### 2. The Three Memoization Tools

**`React.memo`** — memoizes a *component*. Skips re-render if props haven't changed (shallow comparison). Use it on components that:
- Receive stable props
- Are expensive to render
- Render frequently due to a parent that changes for unrelated reasons

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, label }) => {
  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;
});
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a *function reference*. Returns the same function instance across renders unless dependencies change. Use it when passing callbacks as props to memoized children.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [id]);  // only new reference when `id` changes
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a *computed value*. Use it for:
- Expensive calculations you don't want to repeat
- Stabilizing object/array references passed as props

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red', size: 'lg' }), []);
```

**When NOT to memoize:** Don't wrap everything. Memoization has overhead (cache storage + dependency comparison). If a component is cheap to render or doesn't receive unstable props, memoization adds cost without benefit.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

The most common context mistake: putting an inline object as the value. Every time the provider re-renders, a new object is created, and **every consumer re-renders** even if the data didn't change.

```jsx
// Bad — new object reference on every render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fix:** Memoize the context value.

```jsx
// Good
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Better:** Split contexts by update frequency. Put frequently-changing state in one context, stable callbacks in another. Consumers only subscribe to what they need.

---

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before — three unnecessary re-renders on every keystroke:**

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);

  // New function reference every render → Results always re-renders
  const handleSelect = (item) => {
    console.log('selected', item);
  };

  // Recalculated every render even when results haven't changed
  const sortedResults = results
    .slice()
    .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ResultsList items={sortedResults} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Not memoized — re-renders whenever parent does
function ResultsList({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(item => (
    <div key={item.id} onClick={() => onSelect(item)}>{item.name}</div>
  ));
}
```

**After — `ResultsList` only re-renders when `results` actually changes:**

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);

  // Stable reference — only changes if results array changes
  const sortedResults = useMemo(
    () => results.slice().sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)),
    [results]
  );

  // Stable reference — no dependencies, never recreated
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => {
    console.log('selected', item);
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ResultsList items={sortedResults} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Memoized — bails out if props are reference-equal
const ResultsList = React.memo(function ResultsList({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(item => (
    <div key={item.id} onClick={() => onSelect(item)}>{item.name}</div>
  ));
});
```

Now typing in the input updates `query` state, which re-renders `SearchPage`, but `ResultsList` skips its render because `sortedResults` and `handleSelect` references are unchanged.

---

**Decision heuristic:** Profile first with React DevTools "Highlight updates." Optimize only what you can measure. Premature memoization adds complexity and can hide bugs.
